WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.410 --> 00:00:10.700
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: We now move onto the next
section of the agenda on operational performance,

00:00:10.700 --> 00:00:17.580
and the first item on the agenda is the NHS
England Corporate and NHS Performance Report.

00:00:17.580 --> 00:00:21.470
Matthew and Karen I think will share this,
but Matthew, may I please invite you to kick

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:22.470
us off?

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.710
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Thank you very much,
Malcolm; it is a pleasure to be here for my

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:26.710
first meeting.

00:00:26.710 --> 00:00:34.390
It is even more exciting than I expected.

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:37.580
So let me talk about NHS performance.

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:42.570
We are early in the financial year, so the
story is still emerging.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:49.250
Perhaps just to set it in context, demand
remains high in the NHS and is continuing

00:00:49.250 --> 00:00:53.960
to increase, which is why some of what we
have been talking about earlier is so important.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:58.700
So the work that Arvind and Ros are leading
on investing in primary care, the work that

00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:04.070
Ian's team are leading on new models of care,
and the other work that is going on in enhancing

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:11.020
ambulance services and 111, the close work
with social care and the work being done with

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:15.660
technology to try and enhance individuals'
ability to support themselves, is all critical

00:01:15.660 --> 00:01:19.840
to how we manage an NHS going forwards.

00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:26.220
I have had the pleasure of doing 44 STP reviews
with Simon over the last few weeks, where

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:31.640
we have been digging into that and how you
create the transformation to make the NHS

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.720
sustainable going forward.

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:42.560
Last week's reset also reflected the renewed
focus on what needs to be done this year in

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:49.200
order to achieve our promises to patients
and the public.

00:01:49.200 --> 00:01:55.390
So we are now in a position where trusts have
specific trajectories in order to deliver

00:01:55.390 --> 00:02:00.570
those objectives at individual organisation
level.

00:02:00.570 --> 00:02:06.610
In that context we have dropped the financial
penalties that we were applying to individual

00:02:06.610 --> 00:02:13.810
targets, and are supporting NHSI in their
work to help the struggling providers get

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:17.190
to the level that they need to.

00:02:17.190 --> 00:02:22.090
So there is a wide range of work going on
at the moment to hit the performance targets,

00:02:22.090 --> 00:02:28.760
and I think probably we should just touch
on a couple of areas that are in the report.

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:35.310
A&amp;E: from what we can tell from the data from
the first couple of months of the year, growth

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:39.860
in emergency admissions is about on track
with what we expected.

00:02:39.860 --> 00:02:46.200
Some areas are seeing some spikes in emergency
attendances; we need to establish whether

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:51.110
something is actually happening or whether
that is just the natural bouncing up and down

00:02:51.110 --> 00:02:54.510
of data over time.

00:02:54.510 --> 00:03:05.040
Performance is improved on April but is still
below the target level that we would expect.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:12.150
So we have just launched, with NHS Improvement,
the A&amp;E improvement plan which will be guided

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:17.530
by regional A&amp;E delivery boards that are being
created as we speak.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:23.710
We will be focusing on the five things that
we know work, and we will be expecting all

00:03:23.710 --> 00:03:28.490
health systems to put these things in place.

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:33.660
Primary and ambulatory care screening in A&amp;E
departments, so that patients get the right

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:38.540
care quickly, and if they do not need to be
in an A&amp;E department, we can move them onto

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:40.910
the right setting.

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:45.720
Increasing the proportion of 111 calls that
are handled by a clinician, to take pressure

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:50.510
of the ambulance services, off A&amp;E departments
and off GPs if we can close those calls down

00:03:50.510 --> 00:03:54.190
in one call.

00:03:54.190 --> 00:03:58.350
Implementing the ambulance response programme.

00:03:58.350 --> 00:04:02.520
Implementing what we know as best practice
in every hospital for managing the flow of

00:04:02.520 --> 00:04:05.180
patients through hospital.

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:09.420
As an ex hospital chief operating officer
and chief executive, it still takes me by

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:13.980
surprise the huge variation in performance
you will see with organisations that are spending

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:17.310
the same resources on basically managing the
same sorts of patients.

00:04:17.310 --> 00:04:21.299
Some are doing it brilliantly, and others
are doing the opposite of brilliantly.

00:04:21.299 --> 00:04:29.160
So we do need to make sure that everyone is
applying best practice, and NHSI are very

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:30.630
focused on that.

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:35.820
Also, ensuring that discharging best practice
is in place, so that as we work in partnership

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:41.190
with social care, we do not have patients
unnecessarily in hospital, to neither our

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:42.220
benefit nor theirs.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.070
They should be receiving their care in the
right location.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:59.470
So a lot of work going on to drive A&amp;E performance
and improve the care that people receive.

00:04:59.470 --> 00:05:08.940
As has been said elsewhere, the delayed transfers
of care issue is an increasingly significant

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:15.210
one for the NHS, rising generally but rising
particularly amongst patients who are waiting

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:16.220
for social care packages.

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:20.430
It is important: we are a partnership with
social care in delivering this.

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:25.639
If they are unable to provide the set of services
that patients need, then patients end up in

00:05:25.639 --> 00:05:32.720
NHS facilities rather than being cared for
in more appropriate locations.

00:05:32.720 --> 00:05:38.520
The other area I would like to touch on briefly
is around RTT waiting times.

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:44.770
Again, we are seeing improvement on April,
just missed the target by a fraction of a

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:49.770
percentage, but not where we would want to
be at this point in the year.

00:05:49.770 --> 00:05:59.520
So a huge amount of work that needs to be
done, and a series of actions going on both

00:05:59.520 --> 00:06:04.120
outside of hospital trying to make sure that
GPs have the right set of services to refer

00:06:04.120 --> 00:06:10.410
to and we are not getting an unnecessary flow
of referrals in the hospitals, but also within

00:06:10.410 --> 00:06:16.790
hospitals; targeting, for instance, the huge
variation in follow up ratios between hospitals.

00:06:16.790 --> 00:06:24.070
So hundreds of thousands of appointments,
variation between best hospitals in terms

00:06:24.070 --> 00:06:29.100
of their new to follow up ratios versus the
others, which would free up a huge amount

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:34.780
of capacity for new referrals if we could
get everybody to the standard of the upper

00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:37.500
quartile.

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:44.420
So we are working with NHSI on a series of
specific local actions everywhere across the

00:06:44.420 --> 00:06:46.710
country in order to raise that performance.

00:06:46.710 --> 00:06:49.550
So it is early in the year.

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:55.250
We are seeing improvements in many areas,
but there is a lot of work to do, and we are

00:06:55.250 --> 00:07:01.720
very focused now on the move from having a
plan to seeing execution of that plan at ground

00:07:01.720 --> 00:07:02.720
level.

00:07:02.720 --> 00:07:04.450
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you.

00:07:04.450 --> 00:07:10.560
May I invite any comments on that before we
turn to Karen's part of the report?

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:15.229
Could we just focus on the issue that you
have highlighted, which is the variation in

00:07:15.229 --> 00:07:16.540
performance?

00:07:16.540 --> 00:07:23.610
You quite rightly observe in relation to patient
flow that there is a big variation between

00:07:23.610 --> 00:07:27.280
institutions that are using the same resources
to achieve the same ends with basically the

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:35.790
same sort of patient mix, and the same will
be true of A&amp;E, RTT and so on.

00:07:35.790 --> 00:07:39.060
We all know that hospitals are quite complex
organisms and that every one is different,

00:07:39.060 --> 00:07:46.600
but it is still puzzling that, after all these
years going steadily on this problem, we still

00:07:46.600 --> 00:07:49.510
have this extraordinary variation in performance.

00:07:49.510 --> 00:07:53.610
One thing that always strikes me when we get
the data coming to the board is that, although

00:07:53.610 --> 00:07:57.949
we take aggregate data for the whole of England,
it disguises the fact that there are great

00:07:57.949 --> 00:08:03.530
variations between different hospital trusts
and some poor performances, and some of them

00:08:03.530 --> 00:08:06.190
bring down the national average.

00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:11.580
So how satisfied are you that the steps you
have just outlined are going to help us to

00:08:11.580 --> 00:08:18.620
collectivise experience, to provide the mentorship
and to provide the exchange of intelligence

00:08:18.620 --> 00:08:24.449
about how to run the specific services at
the highest level across the whole of the

00:08:24.449 --> 00:08:25.449
provision?

00:08:25.449 --> 00:08:30.940
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I think NHSI are gearing
up to be able to do that, and we are working

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:31.970
closely with them.

00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:35.910
I would say in Jim Mackey they are being led
by the right guy.

00:08:35.910 --> 00:08:42.110
His organisation has continued to be a national
high performer, even whilst he has been down

00:08:42.110 --> 00:08:46.540
working with us nationally, and that is a
very fine example of an organisation that

00:08:46.540 --> 00:08:48.329
just does it.

00:08:48.329 --> 00:08:49.779
You can find these all over the country.

00:08:49.779 --> 00:08:54.889
So if you go to Luton, you will find it being
done; if you go to a Surrey and Sussex hospital

00:08:54.889 --> 00:08:59.970
you see it being done routinely, month in,
month out.

00:08:59.970 --> 00:09:06.560
So I think this will be a high priority for
us working with NHSI to make sure the systems

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:10.379
around hospitals from our point of view are
running optimally, and we are taking the best

00:09:10.379 --> 00:09:12.709
practice and applying it everywhere.

00:09:12.709 --> 00:09:18.140
Then once you get within hospitals, NHSI will
be focusing on that performance.

00:09:18.140 --> 00:09:19.790
I think we know what works now.

00:09:19.790 --> 00:09:26.199
We know what good triage in A&amp;E looks like;
we know what good bed management looks like;

00:09:26.199 --> 00:09:30.529
we know what good preparation for discharge
looks like; we know what good relationships

00:09:30.529 --> 00:09:31.629
with social care looks like.

00:09:31.629 --> 00:09:34.430
We just now need to make it happen flawlessly.

00:09:34.430 --> 00:09:39.259
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: So it is, as we so often
observe, a question of leadership in each

00:09:39.259 --> 00:09:46.829
of these institutions to instil the culture
change that will bring about the performance

00:09:46.829 --> 00:09:47.829
improvements?

00:09:47.829 --> 00:09:49.889
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: I think it is very much
part of leadership.

00:09:49.889 --> 00:09:54.910
If you talk to any of those successful organisations,
the first phone call their chief executive

00:09:54.910 --> 00:09:59.259
makes as they are driving into work in the
morning is asking what the state of the hospital

00:09:59.259 --> 00:10:02.389
is, and the last call they make as they go
home in the evening is checking the state

00:10:02.389 --> 00:10:03.389
of the hospital.

00:10:03.389 --> 00:10:06.319
At any moment during the day, they know what
is going on.

00:10:06.319 --> 00:10:13.249
That ownership, driven by the lead clinicians
throughout the organisation and owned by the

00:10:13.249 --> 00:10:19.129
executive - not behaving like victims of the
circumstances that they find themselves, but

00:10:19.129 --> 00:10:22.740
actually driving performance - is really hard.

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:25.769
Running hospitals is really hard.

00:10:25.769 --> 00:10:28.870
We have lots of new folks coming in and doing
it for the first time, and we need to make

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:33.550
sure we are supporting them and training them,
and getting what we know works spread and

00:10:33.550 --> 00:10:34.949
supported across the system.

00:10:34.949 --> 00:10:40.559
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: I think this is absolutely
top priority for us, working in closest possible

00:10:40.559 --> 00:10:42.360
partnership with NHS Improvement.

00:10:42.360 --> 00:10:49.130
However, it is something where the decline
in constitution standards observance on some

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:55.059
of these issues has been marked in recent
years, and it is very easy to assume that

00:10:55.059 --> 00:10:59.970
the straitened financial circumstances will
lead to further deterioration, when in fact

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:04.610
we know that the remedy is there and that
we can bring about the improvement that we

00:11:04.610 --> 00:11:06.429
need to bring it back up to full compliance.

00:11:06.429 --> 00:11:13.301
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes, and it is the 'both
and' that Jane just talked about: we have

00:11:13.301 --> 00:11:17.369
to both get this year right, and we have to
do the things that need to be in place for

00:11:17.369 --> 00:11:22.509
next year and the year after and the year
after, so that we do not stumble from one

00:11:22.509 --> 00:11:28.309
fire drill to the next, as we try to deal
with it on a crisis by crisis basis rather

00:11:28.309 --> 00:11:30.819
than getting into, this as a managed system.

00:11:30.819 --> 00:11:34.989
Because the public expect us to deliver a
managed system, not a series of incidents.

00:11:34.989 --> 00:11:39.269
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Yes, and our role is to support
the improvement.

00:11:39.269 --> 00:11:40.970
&gt;&gt;Matthew Swindells: Yes, absolutely.

00:11:40.970 --> 00:11:43.499
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Other questions for Matthew?

00:11:43.499 --> 00:11:44.499
No.

00:11:44.499 --> 00:11:47.179
Then, perhaps Karen, may we turn to you?

00:11:47.179 --> 00:11:50.939
&gt;&gt;Karen Wheeler: Yes, thank you, Malcolm.

00:11:50.939 --> 00:11:56.649
This report is really about the assurance
of our delivery against the national programmes

00:11:56.649 --> 00:12:04.040
that we are running, and we obviously continue
to assure those as you would expect.

00:12:04.040 --> 00:12:10.339
Indeed, we have actually discussed a number
of these programmes over the course of today

00:12:10.339 --> 00:12:15.179
and in our previous meeting yesterday as well.

00:12:15.179 --> 00:12:18.569
I was not going to go into a huge amount of
detail.

00:12:18.569 --> 00:12:24.799
The real focus of our assurance at the moment
is on readiness and capability and capacity

00:12:24.799 --> 00:12:30.730
for delivery and implementation, because that
is what we really need to step our game up

00:12:30.730 --> 00:12:33.199
on this year.

00:12:33.199 --> 00:12:38.470
Simon referred to the mental health implementation
plan, for example, as being a very good example

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:43.160
of that, and Jane has talked about learning
disabilities and how that is happening on

00:12:43.160 --> 00:12:44.160
the ground as well.

00:12:44.160 --> 00:12:50.740
There is more to do in some of the programmes
to really step up their capability.

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:52.790
The other thing is three year delivery plans.

00:12:52.790 --> 00:12:59.040
We really need to see plans for the next three
years, not just on an annual basis, and that

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:02.649
is a subject of considerable further work.

00:13:02.649 --> 00:13:07.730
The only area I was going to pick out was
the information and technology portfolio.

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:15.869
A huge amount of work has been done recently
on re framing and re shaping that portfolio.

00:13:15.869 --> 00:13:24.360
Matthew has now taken that responsibility
over, but the key objective of that work was

00:13:24.360 --> 00:13:30.749
to align it and link it much more closely
with the Five Year Forward View and the work

00:13:30.749 --> 00:13:38.480
that needs to happen in delivery, and to ensure
we are allocating our priorities appropriately

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:40.290
into that programme.

00:13:40.290 --> 00:13:48.609
In that context, one of the programmes that
is highlighted as being red, widening digital

00:13:48.609 --> 00:13:56.139
participation, is an area where some progress
has been made recently in terms of agreeing

00:13:56.139 --> 00:13:59.850
the funding for that programme, and we expect
that to move out of red very quickly.

00:13:59.850 --> 00:14:02.529
That was all I was going to say.

00:14:02.529 --> 00:14:05.490
&gt;&gt;Malcolm Grant: Thank you, Karen.

00:14:05.490 --> 00:14:07.269
Anything for Karen?

00:14:07.269 --> 00:14:08.459
No, great.

00:14:08.459 --> 00:14:09.990
Thank you very much, Matthew and Karen.

